WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.900
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:06.029
If you think Making a Murderer was shocking,
you ain’t seen nothing yet.

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:12.130
It’s estimated that every year in Japan
hundreds of people are wrongfully convicted

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.320
for crimes they didn’t commit.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:23.119
Japan is the land of extremes; extreme fashion,
extreme food, extreme game shows.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:29.170
So it only seems right that their jurisdictional
system is extreme too and oh boy is it.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:36.989
Japan boasts an unbelievably high conviction
rate of 99.8% compared to America’s 88%.

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:41.379
But take this figure with a pinch of pickled
ginger because you won’t believe the lengths

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:46.569
Japanese authorities will go to to keep that
figure so high.

00:00:46.569 --> 00:00:50.699
Despite Japan being one of the safest places
on Earth and the world’s third wealthiest

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:57.069
country, its justice system is full of corruption
and believe it or not, psychological torture.

00:00:57.069 --> 00:01:01.909
And no they don’t force people to listen
to J-Pop for days on end whilst force feeding

00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:05.619
shirako to them, don’t Google that.

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:11.079
Suspects are routinely subjected to inhumane
questioning to force them to confess to a

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:16.490
crime that they may or or may not have committed,
just so that the national conviction rate

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.009
can be kept at an impressively high level.

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:24.350
You see, in Japan confessions are believed
to be the ultimate form of evidence to prove

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.280
a person is guilty.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.780
Whereas other countries rely on more tangible
evidence to build a case around, and then

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:36.550
play a game of “who has the more expensive
lawyer” in court, so the jury can make a

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.550
decision.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:44.380
Japanese courts have no jury, a defendant’s
fate is decided by a panel of judges, it’s

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.789
like a really depressing X Factor, in fact,
I’m surprised the Japanese haven’t made

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:53.210
a game show out it yet, oh no wait, America
beat you to that.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:58.790
Obviously the verdict is almost always guilty,
since police interrogators do everything they

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:04.719
can to forcefully extract a guilty confession
out of suspects before it reaches court, whether

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:07.460
they committed the crime or not.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.870
Japanese police have been known to use bizarre
methods to extract confessions, one woman

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:17.240
was made to repeatedly shout her confession
out of the window, another man was forced

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.570
to stomp on the names of his loved ones, by
the interrogators grabbing his ankles.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:26.770
In some kind of twisted game of “do you
feel guilty enough yet”.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:33.080
In most countries such as the UK and USA the
police can only hold a suspect for 24 hours

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:40.180
after arrest, but in Japan police can hold
suspects for up to 23 days without any evidence.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.120
During this time the suspect is not allowed
any outside contact and they aren’t even

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.280
allowed access to an attorney until after
the police have finished their questioning.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.880
Japanese culture places a lot of pride on
one’s family members.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.280
Interrogators use this to their advantage,
during a murder trial in 2013 one suspect

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.480
said that police interrogators repeatedly
told him that his mother wanted him to confess

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.640
or he would be sent to bed without dessert,
but he couldn’t contact his mother to ask

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.420
if she really said this; eventually he confessed
to the murder.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:21.540
He later said he was interrogated non-stop
for five consecutive days, both day and night.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:28.480
He spent 29 years in jail for the murder,
which it turned out he didn’t commit, despite

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.390
confessing to it; he managed to prove his
innocence during a retrial later in his life.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:40.460
In 2007 in a small town in Western Japan,
13 suspects were accused of rigging an election

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.960
via vote-buying.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.490
The suspects were all between 50 and 80 years
old.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.410
They were subject to constant interrogations
for months leading up to the trial.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.640
Six eventually buckled and signed written
confessions, admitting they bought votes with

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.560
liquor and cash.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:03.240
One man died during the trial from stress
and another man and his wife tried to kill

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.330
themselves, twice, but the police stopped
them and arrested them for more questioning.

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:12.370
“Come on now guys you’ve had your fun,
we can’t force a confession out of you if

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.200
your dead, can we?”

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.970
All of the surviving suspects were eventually
found guilty in court despite the police having

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.430
no evidence except from their confessions.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.910
But later they were all acquitted and a court
found that their confessions had been entirely

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.460
fabricated.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:37.810
The judge actually said they had “made confessions
in despair while going through marathon questioning”.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:44.910
In both these cases authorities received absolutely
no punishments for their wrongful convictions.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.570
The Japanese legal system has been heavily
criticised by human rights organisations for

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.450
years, such as the United Nations Human Rights
Committee.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:58.940
The head of Amnesty International in Japan,
Makoto Teranaka says the system is highly

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.460
corrupt and needs to be reformed.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.990
He insists that suspects are subjected to
intense psychological torture and he says

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:11.230
Japan is not a society where you have fair
trials.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:16.500
Things are improving though; some areas of
Japan have recently introduced a US-style

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:17.670
jury system.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:22.470
Although maybe this is not such a good thing
after all, currently Japanese citizens do

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:28.020
everything they can to avoid court, but relaxing
the system could open the doors for more ridiculous

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.060
and unnecessary cases to go to court.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.320
There is definitely an issue with frivolous
litigation in America, like when D.C. based

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:44.840
judge Roy Pearson sued his dry cleaner’s
for $67 million for losing a pair of his pants.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.850
Yeah I’m serious.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:52.630
The court ruled in favour of the dry-cleaners,
and fined Pearson for wasting the court’s

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:53.630
time.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:06:06.670
Pearson totally lost his trousers over the
ruling.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:17.280
Thanks for watching.

